1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:08,767
Picture software as a tree: front end, back end, and
smaller parts. It seems we must build from the
bottom, then integrate upward.

2
00:00:08,767 --> 00:00:14,000
But assumptions about those parts can be wrong. We
build things the result never needed.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,800
Instead, define one small end-to-end scenario with
an automated test. Build enough to pass it; let the
structure grow as needed.

4
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:26,600
As it forms, useful internal tests can cover several
lower parts together.

5
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:32,733
Break a sprint item into small Given, When, Then
scenarios. Each should satisfy a user need on its
own.

6
00:00:32,733 --> 00:00:35,700
Here, we change a setting and check the displayed
result.

7
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:41,833
Take one scenario. Automate its first step using
what already exists. Run it, and see it pass.

8
00:00:41,833 --> 00:00:46,400
Keep that executable evidence. The rest of the
scenario is still unfinished.

9
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:52,833
Add selection. Run it: the test fails because the
dropdown is missing. We have evidence of the missing
work.

10
00:00:52,833 --> 00:00:55,400
The earlier green step stays on the sheet.

11
00:00:55,400 --> 00:01:01,233
A temporary hard-coded dropdown gets us through
sooner. Implementing it properly now is another
choice.

12
00:01:01,233 --> 00:01:05,400
Either way, run the test again. The important result
is still ahead.

13
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:11,167
Add Update. If running it exposes a missing
implementation, that failure tells us the next work.

14
00:01:11,167 --> 00:01:13,133
Follow it into the relevant logic.

15
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:20,933
Protect the existing behavior we touch. Then use a
local test: red, green, refactor. Repeat for the
details this scenario needs.

16
00:01:20,933 --> 00:01:24,733
Return to the acceptance test. Now those earlier
steps pass.

17
00:01:24,733 --> 00:01:30,100
But we still need Then. Add the result check and run
it. The displayed result is wrong.

18
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:32,567
Passing the actions did not finish the scenario.

19
00:01:32,567 --> 00:01:39,733
Five people have been working together. Once the
next work is clear, three can implement the returned
result, using a local test loop.

20
00:01:39,733 --> 00:01:45,167
Two can finish the acceptance automation at the same
time. Both groups serve the same scenario.

21
00:01:45,167 --> 00:01:51,100
Then reunite, integrate, and run the complete
acceptance test. All its steps pass together.

22
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:54,733
Split for brief parallel exploration, and come back
quickly.

23
00:01:54,733 --> 00:02:02,333
Done includes the required implementation and
cleanup. Keep related domain concepts together from
the beginning; make the structure coherent.

24
00:02:02,333 --> 00:02:07,867
Replace needed shortcuts under the checks. Finish
the scenario completely. Only then take the next.

25
00:02:07,867 --> 00:02:13,333
There's also an advanced option: make Then real
first, so the result is already observable.

26
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:16,800
Earlier steps can begin as fakes and become real
gradually.

27
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:23,467
AI can help throughout this work, inside the
scenario and its protective checks. Grow that scope
deliberately.

28
00:02:23,467 --> 00:02:29,033
Acceptance Test Driven Development: work through one
scenario.
